# Contrarian Brief — Against the MWAA Quiet-by-Design Standard

Run: `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
Prepared: 2026-08-07
Role: adversarial second opinion. This brief argues the strongest honest case
against the thesis so the Strategist has to answer it, not tiptoe around it.

## Position

MWAA should not adopt a two-airport quiet-by-design standard. The thesis
concedes the important half of the argument in advance — that "quiet" cannot
mean fewer announcements and must preserve safety, accessibility, and
resilience — and then asks the Council to write a common standard anyway. Once
those concessions are honored, the standard collapses into a restatement of
constraints MWAA already meets: NFPA 72 for voice evacuation intelligibility,
14 CFR Part 382 for accessible information, ACRP-grade acoustic design during
project delivery, and the airline scripting the signatory carriers already
control under lease. A new MWAA-wide document does not add a requirement that
is missing; it adds a governance surface, an audit obligation, and a design-
review veto point that MWAA can already exercise, project by project, without
one.

The advocate should be forced to answer: what does a quiet-by-design standard
require that STI ≥ 0.50 under NFPA 72, plus ACRP Report 175 as reference,
plus ADA/ACAA compliance, plus a competent PA/AV commissioning spec in the
design manual, does not already require? If the answer is "an ambient dBA
target and a reverberation cap," the standard is a technical footnote inside
Design Manual Volume 2, not a board-level initiative. If the answer is a
governance apparatus — announcement categories, frequency caps, tenant script
control, quiet-hour policy, exception process — then the thesis is a fight
with the 2025 Use & Lease, and it should be argued as one.

## 1. The strongest version of the counterargument (7 bullets)

- **The constraint stack already exists and it pulls the opposite direction.**
  NFPA 72 requires an average Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.50 and
  a minimum of 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations inside every acoustically
  distinguishable space [Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/security/mass_notification/mass-notification-intelligibility/].
  "Quiet" specifications and PA intelligibility live on the same
  absorption/reverberation dial. A standard that pushes ambient level and
  reverberation down without simultaneously buying loudspeaker density,
  DSP zoning, and finish reconciliation is a standard that fails Fire Marshal
  review. A standard that funds all of that is a standard that costs whatever
  the design would have cost anyway. Either way, "quiet-by-design" adds no
  net requirement.

- **Every named "silent airport" has kept the announcements that matter.**
  London City's silent-terminal policy, in place since 2008, expressly
  retains announcements for "gate changes at short notice, during disruption
  and when there is a flight information display failure"
  [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over].
  Helsinki, silent since 2015, retains "departure gate and emergency"
  announcements and had to bolt banners under its FIDS telling passengers
  that they now have to track time themselves [Source: same]. Chennai
  triggered a disability-community backlash serious enough that airport
  authority officials publicly conceded "no separate provisions for the
  visually-challenged are available"
  [Source: https://www.deccanchronicle.com/nation/current-affairs/070517/how-will-visually-impaired-flyers-manage-in-silent-terminals.html].
  The advocate is asking MWAA to inherit a policy that no operator has been
  able to run at the strength it was originally marketed.

- **Federal disability law is not neutral on this.** 14 CFR 382.51 requires
  MWAA to ensure "terminal facilities providing access to air transportation
  are readily accessible to and usable by individuals with disabilities" and
  requires captioning "at all times on all televisions and other audio-visual
  displays that are capable of displaying captions"
  [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/14/382.51]. The DOT-required
  channels for flight-critical information — safety, delays, cancellations,
  schedule changes, boarding, connections, gate assignments, paging,
  aircraft changes, emergencies — must be delivered to disabled passengers,
  which in practice means audible and visual [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-II/subchapter-D/part-382].
  Any MWAA standard whose payoff depends on shifting information to
  smartphones will be interpreted as pushing risk onto blind, deaf-blind,
  cognitively disabled, and non-English-speaking travelers, and it will be
  litigated by advocacy organizations that have already taken this fight to
  U.S. carriers [Source: https://simpleflying.com/how-airlines-airports-are-abandoning-blind-travelers/].

- **MWAA does not own the microphone in the concourses.** Airport use-and-
  lease agreements customarily give signatory airlines "the right to approve
  or disapprove proposed airport capital development projects"
  [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/CHAPTER-7-THE-AIRPORT-TENANT-RELATIONSHIP.pdf].
  Under MWAA's residual 2025 Use & Lease, majority-in-interest concurrence
  applies to capital that lands in the rate base. Any quiet-by-design
  standard that changes gate-hold PA design, mandates a new announcement
  governance regime, caps announcement frequency, or reassigns scripting
  authority is a change to airline operating practice — American at DCA and
  United at IAD run those podiums today, and single-agent boarding is
  already an active labor-safety fight
  [Source: https://cwa-union.org/news/releases/gate-and-ramp-agents-american-airlines-subsidiary-report-hazardous-conditions].
  MWAA can require ambient-level and STI targets in the design manual.
  MWAA cannot, unilaterally, tell a gate agent to make fewer calls.

- **DCA cannot afford a silent-boarding experiment.** DCA is slot-controlled
  under the FAA High Density Rule and the statutory perimeter, and in 2025
  the DOT awarded 10 new beyond-perimeter exemptions across five carriers
  under Section 502 of the 2024 FAA Reauthorization
  [Source: `outputs/context/airport-context.md`, §4]. A missed boarding at
  DCA is not a missed boarding at Helsinki — the slot does not wait, the
  aircraft does not re-cue, and the airport is still absorbing the
  operational fragility exposed by the January 2025 midair collision and the
  fall 2025 seven-week federal shutdown, both of which the context packet
  identifies as drivers of DCA's 5.3% traffic decline
  [Source: `outputs/context/airport-context.md`, §6]. Under those
  conditions, a common IAD/DCA standard is the wrong instrument. DCA needs
  more announcement density, better scripting, and better intelligibility —
  not a quiet ceiling.

- **The Saarinen Main Terminal is architecturally unquiet, and that is
  policy.** The IAD Main Terminal is a National Register interior whose
  formal preservation is stated policy under the current modernization
  program [Source: `outputs/context/airport-context.md`, §5]. DOCOMOMO DC
  publicly opposes any revitalization approach that would "compromise the
  essential form, structure, materials, or spatial qualities of Saarinen's
  design" [Source: https://www.archpaper.com/2026/07/president-trump-department-of-transportation-update-dulles-airport/].
  You cannot install broadband absorbers on the coffered concrete shell
  without a preservation fight. A common IAD/DCA acoustic standard therefore
  cannot apply to the most public and most photographed room in the system,
  and the exception is not a footnote — it is the terminal that appears in
  every image of Dulles ever printed. A standard whose exceptions are
  bigger than its rule is a standard that will be honored in the breach.

- **The cheaper alternative already exists and already works.** ACRP
  Research Report 175 provides project-level design guidelines to improve
  PA intelligibility "for all types and sizes of airport terminal
  environments," including finishes, background noise, PA density,
  commissioning, and STIPA verification
  [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrpwebresource2/improving-intelligibility-of-airport-terminal-public-address-systems/].
  ACRP 175 explicitly recommends commissioning that includes before-and-
  after RT60 tests, impulsive-response measurements, and STI evaluations in
  occupied and unoccupied states, with a corrective-action trigger when
  measurements deviate more than 10% from modeled predictions
  [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/11]. MWAA
  can enforce that today by naming ACRP 175 as the reference standard in
  consultant scopes and requiring STIPA commissioning at substantial
  completion. That is a paragraph in each project's specification, not a
  new authority-wide policy instrument.

## 2. Specific scenarios where the thesis produces the wrong outcome

**Scenario A — The DCA irrops cascade.** DCA operates on a single main
runway with slot controls and a heavy business/federal traveler mix. A
storm cell shuts the airfield for 90 minutes. Gate changes, aircraft swaps,
and reboards begin cascading through Terminal 2. Under a quiet-by-design
regime that has already spent political capital reducing announcement
frequency and normalizing app-first notification, gate agents fall back to
non-standard, ad-hoc PA use — the shortcuts are worse than the discipline
they replaced. Passengers who deferred to their phones during the calm hours
are the ones most disoriented in the surge. The standard produced the
opposite of resilience, and the airport wears it in complaint volume, missed-
connection compensation, and DOT consumer complaint filings.

**Scenario B — The ACAA complaint that MWAA can't win.** A blind traveler
without a smartphone misses a boarding at DCA Terminal 2 because the
carrier's app-based push notification did not reach a device the traveler
does not carry, and the gate call complied with the MWAA quiet standard's
frequency cap. The traveler files a DOT complaint under Part 382. The
carrier points at the airport standard. Deccan Chronicle already documented
the "no separate provisions for the visually-challenged" problem in Chennai;
Simple Flying has already documented the pattern of U.S. carriers
"abandoning blind travelers." MWAA is now a named party in an ACAA fight
that its own design manual created. The board will not thank the Council
for producing that headline.

**Scenario C — The airline MII veto.** The 2025 Use & Lease is a two-airport
residual with a 140% coverage target and an $8.6 B rate base pressure on
CPE (rising from $12.77 to a projected $20.71 by 2030 per the 2025A official
statement, per the context packet). United and American read a quiet-by-
design capital line item, add the AV/acoustic delta to their forecast
airline OCC, and decline concurrence for anything above the ACRP-175 baseline.
The standard survives as an aspirational document with no funding path. It
would have been more honest, and more effective, to negotiate PA intelligibility
performance into the technology standards inside consultant scopes for the
CCP work already funded.

**Scenario D — The Saarinen carve-out that eats the standard.** MWAA
publishes a mandatory ambient-noise cap and RT60 target for renovated
terminal space. The Saarinen Main Terminal is exempted because absorption
would alter a National Register interior. Concourse E, already substantially
designed, is grandfathered. Terminal 1 at DCA (1941 Art Deco) is exempted
for parallel preservation reasons. The "common" standard applies to two
concourses at IAD and part of Terminal 2 at DCA — a footprint smaller than
the current MWAA design-manual revision cycle already touches on a rolling
basis.

**Scenario E — The commissioning bill.** MWAA embeds an STI acceptance test
in every renovation over some size threshold. One project fails. The cited
remediation cost from a documented airport terminal case: $2.8 million,
eight weeks of disruption, measured STI 0.32–0.42 against a modeled 0.38
[Source: https://acousplan.com/blog/iec-60268-16-sti-measurement]. Now
multiply across a $9 B CCP and a $20–22.5 B Dulles transformation. The
standard's downside is not that it fails to specify a target — it is that
it locks a target in without a change-control mechanism that can absorb the
inevitable measurement misses without stopping the project.

## 3. Direct quotes and data points a strategist has to concede or answer

1. **NFPA 72, mass-notification intelligibility.** "The intelligibility of an
   emergency communication system (ECS) is considered acceptable if at least
   90 percent of the measurement locations within each acoustically
   distinguishable space (ADS) have a measured STI of not less than 0.45,
   and an average STI of not less than 0.50"
   [Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/security/mass_notification/mass-notification-intelligibility/].
   Any "quiet" specification MWAA adopts must clear this threshold in every
   ADS. The intelligibility requirement is not a soft aspiration; it is a
   safety code that constrains absorption and PA design in the same room.

2. **14 CFR 382.51 on terminal accessibility.** "You must ensure that
   terminal facilities providing access to air transportation are readily
   accessible to and usable by individuals with disabilities, including
   individuals who use wheelchairs" [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/14/382.51].
   Combined with the Part 382 requirement that flight-critical information
   be provided to passengers with disabilities
   [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-II/subchapter-D/part-382],
   any quiet standard that shifts even a fraction of flight-critical
   information off the PA and onto a screen or an app inherits federal
   liability that MWAA does not carry today.

3. **The Chennai concession.** "Officials at airports say no separate
   provisions for the visually-challenged are available"
   [Source: https://www.deccanchronicle.com/nation/current-affairs/070517/how-will-visually-impaired-flyers-manage-in-silent-terminals.html].
   This is the most-cited operator quote from a real silent-airport rollout,
   and it is a confession. MWAA cannot cite Chennai as precedent and
   simultaneously claim its own standard will not produce the same
   accessibility deficit.

4. **The Helsinki concession.** Helsinki Airport is silent, but "at the
   departure gate and when there is an emergency, announcements are still
   broadcast," and the airport had to add banners under its Flight
   Information Displays "informing passengers there will be fewer
   announcements and alerting them to the fact that they need to keep track
   of time" [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over].
   The exemplar model retains the two announcement categories — gate and
   emergency — that account for essentially every operational risk a
   quiet-by-design standard would try to mitigate. What is left is
   background music, general paging, and marketing spots, which MWAA does
   not need a board-level standard to control.

5. **The airline capital veto.** ACI's own tenant-relationship reference
   states: use-and-lease agreements "identify airlines' rights and
   privileges, frequently including the right to approve or disapprove
   proposed airport capital development projects"
   [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/CHAPTER-7-THE-AIRPORT-TENANT-RELATIONSHIP.pdf].
   Any quiet standard that changes gate PA density, mandates additional
   AV/acoustic scope on airline-preferential space, or reassigns
   announcement scripting is a capital and operating change airlines can
   contest under the 2025 agreement. The Strategist has to answer how the
   standard survives MII.

## 4. The concession the advocate should make

The thesis is defensible if it is rescoped from a "standard" to a
**reference-and-commissioning spec** inside the MWAA Design Manual: adopt
ACRP Research Report 175 as the airport-terminal acoustic reference; require
STIPA commissioning at substantial completion for new and renovated
passenger-processing spaces above a defined size threshold; require NFPA 72
compliance to be demonstrated in the occupied state, not simulated; require
FIDS density and captioning to meet or exceed 14 CFR 382.51 in every gate
hold and processing zone; and stay silent on announcement policy, which
belongs to the airlines under the Use & Lease. That is a change MWAA can
make inside the design manual, without a board resolution, without an MII
fight, and without inheriting an ACAA complaint. It is also, notably, a
change the Strategist could recommend without ever using the word "quiet."

If the argument requires the word "quiet" to survive, the argument is a
brand exercise, and the thesis's own "What this is NOT" section already
disallows brand exercises.

## Evidence gaps I could not close

- Whether the current MWAA Design Manual actually specifies NC/RC, RT60,
  STI, or PA density values inside Volumes 2, 3, or their DCA parallels.
  The public record does not disclose the interior of the design manual.
- Whether MWAA has piloted reduced-announcement practice at any concourse
  or hold-room. No public record found.
- Whether the 2025 Use & Lease's MII language reaches design-manual
  changes or passenger-experience capital. The agreement's specific MII
  categories are not public. Both gaps should be raised as open questions
  in the final deliverable, not filled by assumption.

